{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 33, :comments ["<b>it rests</b> — it finds rest in his heart, when he learns it, for the heart is the dwelling-place of the wise." "<b>it is made known</b> — it makes itself known to them, perhaps they will learn it. Another interpretation: it finds rest, in that it does not make itself known to fools," "<b>it is made known</b> — that the fools make it known when they have known it, and it does not rest among them as it rests in the heart of the discerning, as in the verse: A fool utters all his spirit. And it is possible: like the matter of \"and he made known to them the men of Succoth,\" who made known their transgressions by thorns."], :book :proverbs, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {2 "The verse alluded to is Judges 8:14, where the root י-ד-ע is used in the sense of making known, as Ibn Ezra proposes here for תודע in Proverbs 14:33."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Proverbs -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/mishley/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:22:54.099049767Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}